Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your back is getting stronger — keep riding this momentum.",
  "summary": "This was a strong week with a clear focus on your long-term goal of building a bigger back. You dedicated over half your total training volume to back work, and it paid off — your back strength index climbed from 106 to 110, the only muscle group to show genuine progression this period. Total tonnage was substantial at nearly 10,800 kg across 28 sets, which is a solid workload for a 28-year-old male at your bodyweight. The rest of your muscle groups held steady, which is fine given your goal priority, but a few areas worth watching are emerging. Overall, this was a productive and well-directed week.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "on_track",
    "notes": "The back strength index rose from 106 to 110, a meaningful 3.8% gain in a single period, which is excellent and directly aligned with your goal. Biceps held flat at 104, chest at 100, shoulders at 101, and legs at 103 — none of these regressed, but none progressed either. This is acceptable given the heavy back focus, but stagnation in supporting muscle groups like biceps and legs across multiple periods could eventually limit overall development and injury resilience. For now, progressive overload is clearly on track where it matters most."
